#: src/tasks.md:165
msgid "Oneshot tasks"
msgstr ""

#: src/tasks.md:167
msgid ""
"The same task modal opened via `task: spawn` supports arbitrary bash-like "
"command execution: type a command inside the modal text field, and use `opt-"
"enter` to spawn it."
msgstr ""

#: src/tasks.md:169
msgid ""
"The task modal persists these ad-hoc commands for the duration of the "
"session, `task: rerun` will also rerun such tasks if they were the last ones "
"spawned."
msgstr ""

#: src/tasks.md:171
msgid ""
"You can also adjust the currently selected task in a modal (`tab` is the "
"default key binding). Doing so will put its command into a prompt that can "
"then be edited & spawned as a oneshot task."
msgstr ""

#: src/tasks.md:173
msgid "Ephemeral tasks"
msgstr ""

#: src/tasks.md:175
msgid ""
"You can use the `cmd` modifier when spawning a task via a modal; tasks "
"spawned this way will not have their usage count increased (thus, they will "
"not be respawned with `task: rerun` and they won't have a high rank in the "
"task modal). The intended use of ephemeral tasks is to stay in the flow with "
"continuous `task: rerun` usage."
msgstr ""

#: src/tasks.md:178
msgid "More task rerun control"
msgstr ""

#: src/tasks.md:180
msgid ""
"By default, tasks capture their variables into a context once, and this "
"\"resolved task\" is being rerun always."
msgstr ""

#: src/tasks.md:182
msgid ""
"This can be controlled with the `\"reevaluate_context\"` argument to the "
"task: setting it to `true` will force the task to be reevaluated before each "
"run."
msgstr ""

